eps up and does so as you  may know Bartholomew is one of jesus'  twelve apostles but he is mentioned only  briefly in the New Testament Mary says  that she's afraid to speak of this  mystery because if she does fire will  come out of her mouth and destroy the  world then she and Peter dispute who  should lead the group in prayer but  unlike in the Gospels according to  Thomas and Mary Peter does not reject  Mary instead Peter says that as the  tabernacle that once contained God Mary  should lead the prayer but Mary says  that Peter as the pillar of the church  should lead after this goes on for a bit  Mary leads the prayer later in the text  Peter and Mary again argue this time  over who should ask Jesus a question  once again Peter insists that Mary  should and Mary says the Peter should  tired of all this Bartholomew asks the  question so in the quest  of Bartholomew Peter and Mary argue over  who should lead but in this case each  humbly defers to the other  in addition Mary does tell the disciples  about the vision that revealed to her  that she would conceive and give birth  to Christ and as she warned fire comes  out of her mouth and it looks like it  will destroy the world until Jesus  intervenes to stop it the questions of  Bartholomew looks a lot like the gospel  of Mary in both texts mary has special  revealed knowledge that she shares with  the male apostles and she engages in  disputes with Peter but with a  difference here Peter doesn't criticize  Mary he defers to her could the  questions of Bartholomew show that the  Mary of the gospel of Mary is in fact  the Virgin Mary  rather than Mary Magdalene probably not  although we can be fairly certain that  the gospel of Mary comes from the second  century we have no clue about the date  of the questions of Bartholomew it may  be as late as the 6th century in fact  the way that Mary interacts with the  disciples and the questions of  Bartholomew looks lot looks a lot more  like what we see in Apocrypha about the  Virgin Mary from the 3rd and 4th  centuries these are Apocrypha that talk  about Mary's Dormition or her falling  asleep the event that preceded her rise  to heaven a large number of stories  hymns and sermons from late antiquity  and the early Middle Ages described the  last days of Mary's life and they tell  how Mary's soul and body were specially  and miraculously transported to paradise  so that Mary's body was not left in the  ground to suffer corruption and decay  these numerous texts differ in many  respects but they share the fundamental  conviction that as the pure vessel that  had once contained the Son of God  Mary's body could not suffer the same  death and decay that any ordinary body  would moreover as God's mother Mary  enjoys a special relationship with the  Lord one that gives her knowledge of  mysteries beyond that of any other  person  and that enables her to intercede on  behalf of human beings with her divine  son it would take multiple lectures to  go through all of this material so  instead we'll consider two early  apocryphal texts which illustrate two  basic streams of traditions about Mary's  Dormition first the book of Mary's  repose may be from as early as the third  century and every it represents what's  called the palm of the Tree of Life  traditions as the name suggests these  narratives usually feature a palm branch  from the Tree of Life a symbol of  resurrection and they were especially  popular in Western Christianity second  the six books Dormition apocryphon  likely originated in the fourth century  and it represents what's called the  Bethlehem traditions as the name  suggests these narratives take place  primarily in and around Bethlehem let's  look at each of these in turn  the book of Mary's repose was composed  originally in Greek but only fragments  of a Greek text survive instead we have  the complete text only in an ethiop ik  translation the Ethiopian you scripts  are from the 14th and 15th centuries but  Syriac